Keep a Wintun adapter after its tunnel goes down, and reuse it.

Measured on two connects with 0006 in place (charon.log, 2026-10-19):

    19:55:39 installing new virtual IP 172.16.17.11
    19:55:41 created Wintun device: {...3385}            ITM-Office, 2s
    19:56:06 installing new virtual IP 10.0.15.169
    19:56:10 created Wintun device: {...3385}            IKEv2 gw,   4s

Creating a Wintun adapter is a PnP device install, and 0003 did one on every
connect -- one adapter per virtual IP, destroyed with the last holder. That
made it the largest single step of a Windows connect, larger than the whole
IKEv1 + XAuth handshake before it, and one macOS does not have at all: a utun
costs nothing.

Wintun deletes an adapter when its handle is closed, so the cost cannot be
cached anywhere but in the open handle. So:

  del_ip()   when the last holder releases an address, the device is
             unregistered from charon as before, then cleared -- its address
             deleted, and any static route still on it -- and kept, up to
             SPARE_TUNS of them. Past that the old teardown applies.
  add_ip()   takes a kept device before creating one. set_address(),
             set_mtu() and the address install run exactly as they do on a
             fresh device, so nothing downstream can tell the difference.
  create     queues a job that creates one device ahead of the first tunnel,
             so the first connect after the service starts finds one kept
             too. A job, not inline: charon does not answer vici until its
             plugins have loaded, and nothing is waiting on this one.
  destroy    takes the kept devices down with the rest, so shutdown still
             leaves no adapter behind. (Shutdown cancels jobs before it
             unloads plugins, so the warm-up cannot outlive the backend.)

A kept device is not in `tuns`, so it is never mistaken for a holder of a
virtual IP, and it is unregistered before its address changes: the libipsec
router keys its devices by address. Packets Windows sends to an idle adapter
queue in its socketpair and are read, and dropped by libipsec for want of a
policy, once the device carries a tunnel again.

What made reuse safe to ship is the refresh in wait_for_addr(). charon's
interface list only learns addresses from NotifyIpInterfaceChange(), and an
address added to an adapter that is already up changes no interface -- a new
adapter got away with it because its state flips as its address goes on. The
first build of this patch had no refresh and failed exactly that way, twice
in a row (2026-10-19, 20:16 and 20:19):

    reusing adapter '{...3385}'
    virtual IP 172.16.17.11 did not appear on '{...3385}'      4s later
    installing virtual IP 172.16.17.11 failed
    installing route: 10.100.1.220/32 src 192.0.2.10 dev {...3384}

-- the address was on the adapter; charon just never heard of it, dropped the
adapter, and routed the tunnel from the physical address. So the wait re-reads
the adapter list itself between polls, and park_tun() does the same after
clearing an adapter, so its old address leaves charon's view straight away.

A kept adapter must be clean, so clear_tun() reports whether it is: if its
old address or a route on it cannot be removed (anything but "not found"),
park_tun() tears the adapter down as before instead of keeping it. Keeping
it would hand the next tunnel two addresses on one adapter -- the
source-selection fault 0003 exists to prevent -- or a route still pulling the
last tunnel's network into it. A device install on the next connect is the
cheaper failure.

